Apple has rules in place that are supposed to protect iPhone users from unwanted advertising in push notifications and Live Activities, but some apps appear to be flouting these rules, and Apple does not appear to be consistently enforcing them.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/11/Apple-Has-an-iPhone-Notification-Problem-Feature.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1405" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1079488" /><br/>
Apple's <a href="https://developer.apple.com/app-store/review/guidelines/">App Store guidelines</a> do allow iPhone apps to send push notifications for promotions or direct marketing purposes, but only if users have "explicitly opted in to receive them via consent language displayed" in the app, and so long as the app provides a method "for a user to opt out from receiving such messages."<br/>
<br/>
The guideline, in part:<blockquote>Push Notifications should not be used for promotions or direct marketing purposes unless customers have explicitly opted in to receive them via consent language displayed in your app's UI, and you provide a method in your app for a user to opt out from receiving such messages.</blockquote>Unfortunately, there are some examples of apps ignoring this guideline.<br/>
<br/>
On my iPhone in Canada, there are several apps that send me push notifications containing promotions without offering a way to opt out of these promotion-specific notifications, such as Cineplex, McDonald's, and Wendy's.<br/>
<br/>
Canadian food delivery app Skip does offer a setting to turn off promotional notifications, but it has continued to send them to me long after I opted out.<br/>
